<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute uncomplicated cystitis (N30.00)</content>. Typical lower urinary symptoms with no fever or flank pain: uncomplicated cystitis. Rifampin colors the urine orange-red and can make the color pads of a dipstick unreliable, so a clean-catch sample was sent for microscopy and culture rather than relying on the strip; microscopy shows many white cells and bacteria. Pregnancy test negative. Nitrofurantoin is first-line, does not interact with isoniazid or rifampin, and her kidney function is normal. A fluoroquinolone is avoided: these drugs are kept for drug-resistant tuberculosis, and courses of them can mask tuberculosis or select resistance. [S]<list><item>Nitrofurantoin 100 mg twice daily with food for five days.</item><item>Urine culture; result by phone.</item><item>At least two liters of fluid a day, with a jug on her writing desk.</item><item>Return for fever, flank pain, vomiting, or no improvement in two days.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Pulmonary tuberculosis, continuation phase (A15.0)</content>. Every dose taken; no cough, fever or night sweats; no jaundice, nausea or numbness. No interaction between nitrofurantoin and her tuberculosis medicines. [S]<list><item>Continue isoniazid, rifampin and pyridoxine by video-observed therapy without a break.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Official American Thoracic Society/Centers for Disease Control and Prevention/Infectious Diseases Society of America Clinical Practice Guidelines: Treatment of Drug-Susceptible Tuberculosis (2016); International clinical practice guidelines for the treatment of acute uncomplicated cystitis and pyelonephritis in women: a 2010 update by the Infectious Diseases Society of America and the European Society for Microbiology and Infectious Diseases (2010)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Culture result by phone. Preventive visit in five weeks as booked. Return sooner for fever or flank pain. Next scheduled visit 1896-08-24: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
